WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.620
A drug leaves only a few percent of a channel's current.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.640
Does the neuron go quiet?

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.490
Nav1.7 was the obvious target, and the compound tested here has not delivered.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:21.453
One selective blocker was far more potent on half-inactivated channels than on

5
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:25.873
resting ones. Nearly a thousandfold, produced by voltage alone.

6
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:28.583
That is state dependence of block.

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:32.063
It is not selectivity between channel subtypes.

8
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:38.343
In the summarized pharmacology the compound was only elevenfold selective over

9
00:00:38.343 --> 00:00:39.343
another subtype.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:45.567
In a trial in painful diabetic neuropathy it did not significantly improve weekly

11
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:46.567
average pain.

12
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:52.147
The healthy-volunteer study's authors proposed insufficient dose and inadequate

13
00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:54.567
access to the central nervous system.

14
00:00:55.327 --> 00:00:56.627
Those are candidate causes.

15
00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.790
Nav1.8 went differently.

16
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.830
Suzetrigine binds a site away from the pore and stabilizes the closed state.

17
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:10.953
It was highly selective across the human subtypes tested in a recombinant panel.

18
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:16.467
Selectivity in a recombinant panel is not tissue selectivity in a patient.

19
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:22.877
Two surgical trials, after abdominoplasty and after bunionectomy, summed each

20
00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:25.297
patient's pain relief over 48 hours.

21
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.427
Suzetrigine beat placebo in both.

22
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:33.407
Neither trial demonstrated superiority over the opioid comparator.

23
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:39.737
A failed superiority test is not equivalence, and one trial's comparator analysis

24
00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:40.997
ran the other way.

25
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:48.477
Approval in the United States followed in January 2025, for moderate-to-severe acute

26
00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:49.477
pain in adults.

27
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.370
The two drug results came from different pain conditions.

28
00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:55.930
They are not a comparison of targets.

29
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.283
Now the channel itself.

30
00:01:59.123 --> 00:02:03.303
A one-to-one model was fitted to the recombinant current at body temperature.

31
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:08.817
The temperature of the rig changes that fitted number by more than fivefold.

32
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:14.447
At a bath leaving only a few percent of that recombinant current, what does a human

33
00:02:14.447 --> 00:02:15.447
neuron do?

34
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.750
Generally, it keeps firing.

35
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.950
During depolarizing current injection, most tested neurons kept firing repetitively.

36
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.190
Firing was generally reduced, but not eliminated.

37
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:35.423
The peak and the shoulder came down.

38
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:38.863
Threshold and the initial upstroke were largely spared.

39
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:43.123
The voltage-clamp analysis suggested two reasons.

40
00:02:44.023 --> 00:02:48.323
In many of these neurons the Nav1.8 current is very large.

41
00:02:49.203 --> 00:02:52.183
Near-complete block still leaves nanoampere-level current.

42
00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:58.867
And the other channel, Nav1.7, dominates the initial spikes in these recordings.

43
00:02:60.000 --> 00:03:03.380
So a channel-current calculation is not neuronal silence.

44
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.520
And it is certainly not a dose.
